> Here we go again... I've been doing a bit of work on the design of an
> OOP nethack, and there's one thing I just can decide: How do you model
> item / item interactions? For example, dipping a unicorn horn in a
> potion. If it's healing, nothing happens. If it's hallucination, the
> potion turns to water.  Which object should contain this knowledge?
> This gets even more difficult if you're not sure what all the objects
> in the game are, as we were discussing previously.

It sounds like you want generic functions.  The Common Lisp Object
System (CLOS) does this the Right Way, and there are implementations for
Python, too (not that the Python implementations are wrong, just that
CLOS works that way right out of the box).

> A visitor-pattern style double-dispatch seems too restrictive to me.
> Perhaps a registry of interactions, storing the first item, the
> action, the second item, and a function to call when that happens...
> Would be tedious, though.

Your "registry" is almost a vague description of multi-method dispatch
with generic functions.  The real advantage of generic functions and the
proper dispatch mechanism (especially in languages like Python and Lisp)
is that different objects can specialize a standard behavior in
extremely flexible ways, up to and including run-time addition of brand
new objects and interactions.

